1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,864
Every answer an AI model gives you is built one token at a time.

2
00:00:04,864 --> 00:00:09,955
Behind every token sits a chain of silicon, memory, networking and power.

3
00:00:10,025 --> 00:00:16,511
This is the journey from GPU to token, and why the token has become the unit of AI economics.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,510
It starts with the accelerator.

5
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:34,309
An NVIDIA H100 packs 132 streaming multiprocessors with fourth-generation Tensor Cores: roughly one petaFLOP of dense BF16 compute, and two at FP8.

6
00:00:34,403 --> 00:00:43,508
But compute is only half the story. 80 GB of HBM3 feeds those cores at 3.35 terabytes per second.

7
00:00:43,602 --> 00:00:51,487
H200 raises that to 141 GB at 4.8 TB/s, and Blackwell goes further, with native FP4.

8
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,607
One GPU is never enough.

9
00:00:54,607 --> 00:01:05,058
Inside an HGX server, eight GPUs are joined by NVLink and NVSwitch at 900 GB/s per GPU, so they act as one large accelerator.

10
00:01:05,142 --> 00:01:16,430
Across servers, a rail-optimised 400-gigabit InfiniBand or RoCE fabric, one NIC per GPU, links thousands of GPUs into a single cluster.

11
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:33,236
Add parallel storage for data and checkpoints, and serious power and cooling: an eight-GPU server draws around ten kilowatts; a rack-scale Blackwell NVL72 needs around 120, with direct liquid cooling.

12
00:01:34,333 --> 00:01:39,918
Building this is capital-intensive, and GPU generations turn over every year or two.

13
00:01:39,968 --> 00:01:45,851
GPU-as-a-Service turns that infrastructure into an elastic, multi-tenant platform.

14
00:01:45,901 --> 00:01:50,056
Bare-metal nodes and GPU-passthrough VMs at the base.

15
00:01:50,106 --> 00:01:54,688
Kubernetes with the GPU Operator, or Slurm, for orchestration.

16
00:01:54,738 --> 00:02:00,984
Multi-Instance GPU splits one H100 into up to seven isolated slices.

17
00:02:01,034 --> 00:02:06,000
DCGM telemetry tracks utilisation, memory, power and errors.

18
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:08,840
Customers consume it by the GPU-hour.

19
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:14,418
With TM GPUaaS, on sovereign, Malaysia-hosted infrastructure.

20
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:19,281
But a language model doesn't think in GPU-hours.

21
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:20,884
It thinks in tokens.

22
00:02:20,964 --> 00:02:33,467
A byte-pair-encoding tokenizer splits text into sub-word units from a fixed vocabulary: about 100 thousand entries in GPT-4's cl100k, 200 thousand in o200k.

23
00:02:33,548 --> 00:02:38,277
Each token becomes an integer ID, then an embedding vector.

24
00:02:38,357 --> 00:02:41,643
And tokenization is not language-neutral.

25
00:02:41,723 --> 00:02:50,380
The same sentence in Bahasa Melayu costs 36 percent more tokens with o200k, and 82 percent more with cl100k.

26
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:54,948
More tokens means more compute, more latency, more cost.

27
00:02:56,033 --> 00:02:58,140
Inference runs in two phases.

28
00:02:58,190 --> 00:03:01,087
Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel.

29
00:03:01,137 --> 00:03:04,439
It is compute-bound, and it sets time-to-first-token.

30
00:03:04,489 --> 00:03:10,948
Decode then generates one token per step, and every step streams the full set of weights from HBM.

31
00:03:10,998 --> 00:03:13,468
That makes decode memory-bandwidth-bound.

32
00:03:13,518 --> 00:03:18,847
Llama 3.1 8B in BF16 is 16 GB of weights.

33
00:03:18,897 --> 00:03:28,180
At 3.35 TB/s, a single stream tops out near 200 tokens per second, while the Tensor Cores sit mostly idle.

34
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:35,458
To avoid recomputing attention, every token's keys and values are cached.

35
00:03:35,458 --> 00:03:43,602
For Llama 3.1 70B, with 80 layers and grouped-query attention, that's 320 KB per token in BF16.

36
00:03:43,687 --> 00:03:54,917
One 128K-token context needs about 40 GB of KV cache: memory that competes directly with batch size, and therefore with throughput.

37
00:03:56,033 --> 00:03:58,482
This is where the serving stack earns its keep.

38
00:03:58,532 --> 00:04:02,580
Continuous batching shares each weight read across many requests.

39
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:07,724
PagedAttention allocates KV cache in blocks, eliminating fragmentation.

40
00:04:07,774 --> 00:04:10,351
FlashAttention keeps attention on-chip.

41
00:04:10,401 --> 00:04:14,386
FP8 and FP4 quantisation cut bytes per weight.

42
00:04:14,436 --> 00:04:19,466
Speculative decoding drafts several tokens and verifies them in one pass.

43
00:04:19,516 --> 00:04:27,746
Prefix caching reuses shared prompts, and disaggregated serving puts prefill and decode on separately tuned GPU pools.

44
00:04:27,796 --> 00:04:35,670
Engines like vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM combine these for order-of-magnitude gains.

45
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:45,869
So the real output of a GPU is tokens per second, delivered within latency targets for time-to-first-token and inter-token latency.

46
00:04:45,919 --> 00:04:48,986
That gives the core equation of AI economics.

47
00:04:49,036 --> 00:04:56,285
Cost per million tokens equals the GPU-hour cost, divided by tokens per hour, times one million.

48
00:04:56,335 --> 00:05:04,757
Illustratively: at ten dollars per GPU-hour and 200 tokens per second, that's nearly fourteen dollars per million tokens.

49
00:05:04,807 --> 00:05:11,458
Optimise the same GPU to 5,000 tokens per second, and it drops to about 56 cents.

50
00:05:11,508 --> 00:05:12,805
Same hardware.

51
00:05:12,855 --> 00:05:15,076
Twenty-five times cheaper tokens.

52
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:23,757
That is why the industry is moving up the stack: from selling hardware, to selling GPU-hours, to selling tokens.

53
00:05:23,757 --> 00:05:34,764
Model-as-a-Service endpoints with OpenAI-compatible APIs, metered per input and output token, let customers pay for intelligence produced, not for idle capacity.

54
00:05:34,833 --> 00:05:42,285
For the provider, the metric that matters becomes tokens per second per GPU, and ultimately, tokens per watt.

55
00:05:43,367 --> 00:05:46,806
From silicon, to GPUaaS, to tokens.

56
00:05:46,806 --> 00:05:53,388
TM GPUaaS: sovereign GPU cloud for AI, built for the token economy.
